/E (Large Language Models \(LLMs\) are fundamentally prediction engines, not knowledge bases. They generate text by predicting the most statistically likely next word based on patterns learned from training data. They do not understand truth, they predict plausibility. When the model encounters a gap in its training data or faces an ambiguous query, the mathematics of its softmax function force it to guess the most plausible-sounding continuation, bridging gaps in logic with seamless fiction.For computer science engineers, the mandate is clear: we can no longer treat these hallucinations as traditional software "bugs" that can be patched. Because this stochastic behaviour is mathematically inevitable, absolute reliability must be engineered through external architectural oversight.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
446 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 111 0 R
/T (Engineering Reliability:)
/E (Engineering Reliability:)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
