472 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (At the center of all this was Nahida, imprisoned in the Sanctuary of Surasthana, dismissed as powerless by the very scholars who claimed to protect wisdom. Yet Nahida saw what the sages could not. Knowledge without compassion becomes arrogance. Information without understanding becomes emptiness. And a nation that worships answers too much may forget how to ask the right questions.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
473 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
474 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (One of her most important choices came after the battle. She shut down the Akasha system. This decision mattered because the Akasha had become more than a tool. It had become a crutch. For generations, the people of Sumeru had grown used to receiving knowledge instantly, depending on the system instead of exercising their own memory, judgment, and curiosity. The nation that loved wisdom had, in some ways, become distant from the struggle that true understanding requires.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
475 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (That is what makes the Akasha feel so close to our own world.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (Today, people turn to AI in much the same way the people of Sumeru turned to their terminals.They ask it to explain, summarize, write, organize, and decide. AI can be helpful, just as the Akasha was helpful. It can widen access to knowledge, save time, and reduce effort. But the danger begins when help becomes dependence.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
