490 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 113 0 R
/E (The more people rely on a system to remember for them, think for them, or create for them, the easier it becomes to let their own abilities grow dull.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
491 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 113 0 R
/E (The Akasha was never evil by nature. Its tragedy came from the way it reshaped the habits of an entire society. It made knowledge immediate, but perhaps too easy. It connected everyone, but also made them vulnerable to control by those who ruled the system. In the wrong hands, a network meant to share wisdom became a structure for surveillance, manipulation, and ambition.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
492 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 113 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
493 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 113 0 R
/E (The fall of the Akasha was not the end of knowledge in Sumeru. It was the beginning of something more human. People would have to remember, study, question, and learn again without leaning so heavily on the voice in their ears. That change was not as effortless as the old system had been, but it was freer.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
